 "published": false,
 "title": "The Zen Journey-KAMAKURA",
 "description": "<p style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:#57646f;margin-top:0.5em;margin-bottom:0.5em\">Kamakura has kept its temples for eight hundred and fifty years, and its</p><p style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:#57646f;margin-top:0.5em;margin-bottom:0.5em\">stillness for just as long. This tour is built around that stillness — not a</p><p style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:#57646f;margin-top:0.5em;margin-bottom:0.5em\">checklist of sights, but a slow pass through three of the city’s five great Zen</p><p style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:#57646f;margin-top:0.5em;margin-bottom:0.5em\">temples, with a guide who can explain what you’re looking at, and enough</p><p style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:#57646f;margin-top:0.5em;margin-bottom:0.5em\">quiet, unhurried time to actually look.</p><p style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:#57646f;margin-top:0.5em;margin-bottom:0.5em\">The Five Mountains, or Gozan, are the five preeminent Rinzai Zen temples</p><p style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:#57646f;margin-top:0.5em;margin-bottom:0.5em\">whose foundations were laid under the Kamakura shogunate and which</p><p style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:#57646f;margin-top:0.5em;margin-bottom:0.5em\">were later ranked at the highest tier by the Muromachi shogunate:</p><p style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:#57646f;margin-top:0.5em;margin-bottom:0.5em\">Kencho‑ji, Engaku‑ji, Jufuku‑ji, Jochi‑ji, and Jomyo‑ji.</p><p style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:#57646f;margin-top:0.5em;margin-bottom:0.5em\">You’ll sit for a short zazen at Engaku‑ji, wander the seasonal grounds of</p><p style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:#57646f;margin-top:0.5em;margin-bottom:0.5em\">Jochi‑ji, and take matcha before a dry rock garden at Jomyo‑ji, before lunch</p><p style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:#57646f;margin-top:0.5em;margin-bottom:0.5em\">closes the morning at a place suited to your taste.</p><p style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:#57646f;margin-top:0.5em;margin-bottom:0.5em\">Everything is arranged in advance. All you need to bring is a willingness to slow down.<br /><br /><br /><strong>Practical Notes<br /></strong>Comfortable walking shoes are recommended — the approach to several</p><p style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:#57646f;margin-top:0.5em;margin-bottom:0.5em\">temples includes stone steps. The tour is all‑inclusive: transport, admission,</p><p style=\"font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:#57646f;margin-top:0.5em;margin-bottom:0.5em\">lunch, and your guide’s company throughout.</p>",
 "excerpt": "A private , guided passag e through three meditation, templ e grounds wa l ked be suit, with an English-speaking guide of the Kamakura Five Mountains — Engaku-ji, Jochi-ji and Jomyo-ji. S eated fore the crowds, matcha over a dry l andscape garden, and a l",
